#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189404 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\mu_{X \\mid y}\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}(X - \\mu_{X \\mid y} \\big)^2 \\, & \\, Y=y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs with the discrete case, this definition can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X \\mid y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X^2 \\, & \\, Y=y \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 \\, f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nand $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$. Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ for continuous $X$.\n\nWhen we select a particular value of $y$, the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm {Var}\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngives us a *function* of $y$.\n\nWe have an analogous definition for the **conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x{:}$**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\mu_{Y \\mid x} = \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=x \\big\\end{bmatrix}$ is the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$,\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y^2 \\, & \\, X=x \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y$, and\n- $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc60678b-fd8c-537f-99be-cee66503c859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6444 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189410 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables. It is known that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, 1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{4y^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\n\\mu_{Y \\mid 1}=\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nWhat is the conditional variance\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=1\\big\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y^2 \\, & \\, X=x \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 1)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}y^{2} \\cdot 4y^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}4y^{5}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{2y^{6}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid 1}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{75}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "51fd1dbf-d36f-5e8c-b62b-ede292dae6da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6490 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
